Notebook[{
Cell["WolframExample: Rust libraries from the Wolfram Language", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"eeca1d93-825c-4ca7-bc24-0e50488b9acd"],

Cell["\<\
This notebook exercises the WolframExample paclet, whose functions are \
implemented in Rust (see WolframExample/Libs). Evaluate it top to bottom.

If the setup section reports a missing paclet, build the library first:
    cd WolframExample && cargo wl build\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9f0db15a-fd13-4018-acee-acea818ba9ba"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Setup", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"303bc46a-1361-47b2-8b17-3cb0458dea6e"],

Cell["\<\
Both paclets \[LongDash] the hand-written WolframExample and the generated \
WolframExampleLib \[LongDash] sit in this notebook's parent directory, so one \
PacletDirectoryLoad registers both.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a9e0f31f-61ec-468a-a865-aebc7b76e54f"],

Cell["PacletDirectoryLoad[ParentDirectory[NotebookDirectory[]]]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"6828de78-584e-4096-877c-88bb27704290"],

Cell["PacletObject[\"WolframExample\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"9775f586-fea2-49af-b6e9-ea3cc98d36ab"],

Cell["\<\
The library paclet holds nothing but the compiled dylibs and a generated \
Functions.wl loader:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fe698b15-e7e6-468c-8f88-b372e139590d"],

Cell["PacletObject[\"WolframExampleLib\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"469e8191-ad96-49c2-b0b7-f1a691fd8fd1"],

Cell["Needs[\"WolframExample`\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b35eff9c-5c59-44a8-8b5e-72406e68282e"],

Cell["Everything the package exports:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"df201ff8-82d5-46aa-924c-8ea801f25389"],

Cell["?WolframExample`*", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"330a82fd-7591-4c81-b54a-81119647793e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Math", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d8732d2a-a0eb-4340-b349-821308e942c9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Scalars and arrays", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"14d91352-dd0e-4fc3-b2f3-235900fcedc9"],

Cell["\<\
A plain Rust function over two f64s. Arguments and result cross as raw \
MArgument scalars \[LongDash] no serialization at all.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0196a277-dba8-4ae2-8780-1163b1556d6c"],

Cell["ExampleAdd[2, 3]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"77e9e0c1-4b99-4f0d-8020-6277bbe839a8"],

Cell["\<\
NumericArrays are read zero-copy: Rust sees the kernel's own memory, no copy \
and no conversion. Lists are packed into a Real64 NumericArray by the wrapper \
first.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f92f7b1a-8fad-4cb2-a0be-048ac6d4e409"],

Cell["ExampleDot[{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a560f447-df51-4e21-8ae5-be4cccb7cde3"],

Cell["\<\
ExampleDot[
    NumericArray[Range[1., 100000.], \"Real64\"],
    NumericArray[Range[100000., 1., -1.], \"Real64\"]
]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e8d27b12-318e-48ac-9fe6-c2330c60b14c"],

Cell["\<\
The Rust side accumulates into 8 independent lanes so that LLVM can vectorize \
the loop (see Libs/math/src/lib.rs) \[LongDash] about twice the throughput of \
the naive left-to-right sum. Timed against the kernel's own Dot, which calls \
into BLAS; a Speedup above 1 means the Rust library won.

Two things decide what you see here. Time only the call, not the packing: \
both arrays are converted to NumericArrays first, since passing plain lists \
spends about three times the call itself on the conversion. And build with    \
 cargo wl build --release
\[LongDash] cargo wl test leaves an unoptimized build in place, which loses \
by an order of magnitude.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2e7d85a2-a249-4161-9927-8ec7b36ad848"],

Cell["\<\
With[
    {a = Range[1., 100000.], b = Range[100000., 1., -1.]},
    {na = NumericArray[a, \"Real64\"], nb = NumericArray[b, \"Real64\"]},
    {rust = First @ RepeatedTiming @ ExampleDot[na, nb],
     kernel = First @ RepeatedTiming @ Dot[a, b],
     packing = First @ RepeatedTiming @ ExampleDot[a, b]},
    <|
        \"Rust\" -> Quantity[rust, \"Seconds\"],
        \"Kernel\" -> Quantity[kernel, \"Seconds\"],
        \"Speedup\" -> kernel / rust,
        \"RustFromLists\" -> Quantity[packing, \"Seconds\"]
    |>
]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"66b16d1d-a18f-487f-9215-0a2d5562dae0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Structs and enums", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"8e9b0fa9-3dd7-48d8-b8e9-b857ab487b60"],

Cell["\<\
Structured arguments cross as WXF. A Rust struct is an Association keyed by \
its field names; the wrapper here presents Wolfram-style capitalized keys.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f6dfe0a4-9bb1-49f0-b0e4-9e4511afc48a"],

Cell["ExampleSymmetricPoint[<|\"X\" -> 10, \"Y\" -> 20|>]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"58bc953b-9dd2-416e-adcd-91c471d3639d"],

Cell["\<\
ExampleArea goes through a Rust enum \[LongDash] one function, one match, two \
shapes:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f6ddbd7a-7c66-44c6-908f-235e9f29ca29"],

Cell["ExampleArea[<|\"Radius\" -> 1|>]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"34af1000-0d01-4e2f-b086-1da98a500b0f"],

Cell["ExampleArea[<|\"Width\" -> 10, \"Height\" -> 20|>]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"faf90bcf-574f-48fa-842e-98c0f6c6d967"],

Cell["Table[ExampleArea[<|\"Radius\" -> r|>], {r, 1, 5}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"d425caa7-5efb-450e-8f55-2849760f5dfa"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Errors", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"d5590665-ddb8-4171-b4e3-7b469d2b641b"],

Cell["\<\
A Rust Result<f64, String> comes back as {\"Ok\", value} or {\"Err\", \
message}; the wrapper turns the error branch into a Failure.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d7b9d903-a724-4171-95bb-a010b1d8bcbb"],

Cell["ExampleSafeDivide[10, 4]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"3f80f095-464e-4752-9a94-1fb32448d970"],

Cell["ExampleSafeDivide[1, 0]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"4a849883-5840-4282-874e-5d74a47c0e70"],

Cell["So the usual Failure handling applies:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"db827967-5273-4bda-87cd-48e74a6ea470"],

Cell["\<\
FailureQ /@ {ExampleSafeDivide[1, 2], ExampleSafeDivide[1, 0]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"be3c4b68-f87c-42d3-9b4a-07b372d71f46"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["URL encoding", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"0cc4c749-5e65-45c4-bcc2-a4e638552681"],

Cell["\<\
ExampleURLEncode and ExampleURLDecode are drop-in equivalents of the kernel's \
own URLEncode and URLDecode, built on percent-encoding \[LongDash] the RFC \
3986 half of the Rust url crate. Same character set, same uppercase hex, same \
treatment of a bare + on the way back.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"49685b48-cea4-4393-9881-48d6c8de740b"],

Cell["ExampleURLEncode[\"Hello, world!\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"ed73dd5f-0c97-4644-93df-684048ce3dc8"],

Cell["ExampleURLDecode[\"Hello%2C%20world%21\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e2920a25-8d28-46b3-94d4-cc7963b5b025"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Lists are the real interface", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"7677ffa1-02ef-4e9c-95bf-592d5ac6c797"],

Cell["\<\
The Rust functions take a list and return a list, and that is all they take: \
crossing the library boundary costs one WXF round trip, so encoding n strings \
should be one call rather than n. The single-string form above is a two-line \
wrapper in Kernel/Functions/URL.wl \[LongDash] wrap, call, unwrap.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e9903573-dc55-4db5-95ab-4d6447d3ca29"],

Cell["\<\
ExampleURLEncode[{\"a+b\", \"7 x + 5 y\", \"Kurt G\\[ODoubleDot]del\"}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"fee60081-bed0-41d8-b162-4190ffd3d11a"],

Cell["\<\
Decoding is deliberately asymmetric, exactly as the kernel's is: a literal + \
is a space, while %2B is a plus sign. Anything that is not a complete escape \
is left alone.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d0ee0d57-73cf-40f0-8dda-6b3d13419125"],

Cell["ExampleURLDecode[{\"a+b\", \"a%2Bb\", \"a+b%2Bc\", \"100%\", \
\"%zz\"}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"170fb219-2270-478e-b0d8-c9236ea09dce"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Agreement with the kernel", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"bbef2d69-9803-4c1f-a236-469c081ec19f"],

Cell["\<\
Not a spot check: every character in the Basic Multilingual Plane, encoded \
both ways and compared. Lone surrogates are left out because they have no \
UTF-8 form at all \[LongDash] a Wolfram string can hold one, a Rust str \
cannot, and the library boundary rejects the payload before any of this code \
runs.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ec6b558b-a038-473f-b015-85af2c64d275"],

Cell["\<\
bmp = FromCharacterCode /@ Join[Range[0, 16^^D7FF], Range[16^^E000, 2^16 - 1]];
Length[bmp]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"11e9ca5b-efc4-4e2f-b79c-aba76036ee89"],

Cell["\<\
Counts[MapThread[SameQ, {ExampleURLEncode[bmp], URLEncode /@ bmp}]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"62f62f37-b030-4a6f-b10a-c060ab7163af"],

Cell["\<\
Counts[MapThread[SameQ, {ExampleURLDecode[URLEncode /@ bmp], bmp}]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"dd79842e-43a7-4169-bffe-1f55b540d319"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Benchmark", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"a793242e-1598-4efb-a2ef-54eac9538fef"],

Cell["\<\
Both directions, against the kernel's own implementation, over lists of \
40-character strings drawn from an alphabet that is mostly punctuation and \
non-ASCII \[LongDash] so almost every character needs escaping. Speedup above \
1 means the Rust library won.

At n = 1 the WXF round trip is most of the call and the margin is small; by n \
= 100 it has amortized away and the ratio settles. As with ExampleDot, build \
with
    cargo wl build --release
\[LongDash] cargo wl test leaves an unoptimized build in place.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"458ab54f-7450-45fb-9c09-68f2e41d6858"],

Cell["\<\
SeedRandom[42];
alphabet = Join[Characters[\"abcdefgh ?&=/:%\"], {\"\\[ODoubleDot]\", \
\"\\[Alpha]\"}];
sample[n_] := Table[StringJoin[RandomChoice[alphabet, 40]], n];\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c34d2a6c-1e14-4c56-b51b-7cfc062098e1"],

Cell["\<\
row[n_] := Module[{data = sample[n], encoded},
    encoded = URLEncode /@ data;
    <|
        \"n\" -> n,
        \"EncodeSpeedup\" -> First[RepeatedTiming[URLEncode /@ data]] /
                           First[RepeatedTiming[ExampleURLEncode[data]]],
        \"DecodeSpeedup\" -> First[RepeatedTiming[URLDecode /@ encoded]] /
                           First[RepeatedTiming[ExampleURLDecode[encoded]]]
    |>
];
Dataset[row /@ {1, 10, 100, 1000, 10000}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"69993613-d3b7-4585-a2b7-db876824439c"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["DuckDB", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"aaba0ada-2e19-457f-a89a-6c2636aa9375"],

Cell["\<\
An entire embedded SQL engine, statically linked into the library \[LongDash] \
nothing to install. Query results come back as Arrow and are decoded into a \
Tabular.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4d5b2896-641c-4da6-ba7b-84692c2f9d30"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Scoped connections", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"96d35583-767b-4cdb-b61a-c8d1e8130a43"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBExecute opens a connection, applies the function to it, and closes it \
again \[LongDash] even if the body fails.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"42e3b1f4-b761-4378-bf69-bf89f9ff24e6"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBExecute[\"duckdb://\", DuckDBQuery[#, \"SELECT 42 AS answer\"] &]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"0bd24134-cc2e-4f4f-95b9-23890d2f5932"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBExecute[\"duckdb://\", DuckDBQuery[#,
    \"SELECT n::INTEGER AS id,
            (n * 1.5)::DOUBLE AS score,
            'item_' || n AS label,
            n % 2 = 0 AS is_even
     FROM generate_series(1, 5) t(n)\"] &]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"8bd93dfe-dce6-4c72-b17d-45f39885f1e0"],

Cell["\<\
The result is an ordinary Tabular, so the usual operations work:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"13034c9c-5cfc-4b3d-9287-db92c8d5dd81"],

Cell["\<\
tab = DuckDBExecute[\"duckdb://\", DuckDBQuery[#,
    \"SELECT n FROM generate_series(1, 100) t(n)\"] &];
{Length[tab], Total[tab[[All, \"n\"]]]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"86c3388e-d83f-4d6b-8cda-43f9f1d03266"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Parameters", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"2f1b7fb9-7ad0-4bbe-81c2-ce29b4de0840"],

Cell["\<\
Values are bound to the ? placeholders in order, so nothing is interpolated \
into the SQL string.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"89f1857a-d19f-4ba4-9e49-45f9fa0f2120"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBExecute[\"duckdb://\", DuckDBQuery[#,
    \"SELECT n FROM generate_series(1, 5) t(n) WHERE n > ?\", {2}] &]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"deff0abf-4c76-4be2-92fd-6d13f8baa7fe"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBExecute[\"duckdb://\", DuckDBQuery[#,
    \"SELECT ?::INTEGER + ?::INTEGER AS total\", {20, 22}] &]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"870ecc9f-e64d-4f8a-8be4-54508a5950a4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Explicit connections", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"2db3b46d-a4b2-4a8c-b9fb-5e7ab88fd8e0"],

Cell["\<\
For several statements against the same database, hold the handle yourself. \
Here an on-disk file, which persists after disconnecting.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"60f61491-b65f-4d40-84a7-4172f7c0dc34"],

Cell["\<\
file = FileNameJoin[{$TemporaryDirectory, \"wolfram-example.duckdb\"}];
Quiet[DeleteFile[file]];
conn = DuckDBConnect[\"duckdb:///\" <> file]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"afee97bc-b978-4759-b29b-df16228c4858"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBQuery[conn,
    \"CREATE TABLE fruit AS SELECT * FROM (VALUES (1, 'apple'), (2, 'pear')) \
v(id, name)\"]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"069d63d4-2500-4f98-a5ce-4a651f3799ec"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBQuery[conn, \"INSERT INTO fruit VALUES (?, ?)\", {3, \"plum\"}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"68af922d-422d-4b3e-bb3c-eeb726918f66"],

Cell["DuckDBQuery[conn, \"SELECT * FROM fruit ORDER BY id\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"8615b9ef-4819-473b-9e42-2f2d041c40c4"],

Cell["DuckDBDisconnect[conn]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"2da6f5c7-c2fd-4eef-94dd-fc74f86980eb"],

Cell["Reopening the same file shows the rows are still there:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"eee91175-64df-41d4-b3b3-789f30278cb2"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBExecute[\"duckdb:///\" <> file,
    DuckDBQuery[#, \"SELECT count(*) AS rows FROM fruit\"] &]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e2256fbe-dfd0-429e-994e-0bc5202e42c7"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Failures", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"598e64e9-674d-4ede-bdf6-6268df7c0150"],

Cell["\<\
Errors from the engine arrive as Failure objects carrying DuckDB's own \
message, and propagate on their own.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9d4aceb6-da19-4789-8a32-0a02c7f6b442"],

Cell["\<\
DuckDBExecute[\"duckdb://\", DuckDBQuery[#, \"SELECT * FROM no_such_table\"] \
&]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"67114ebe-6437-4169-811c-64a278e3a7ea"],

Cell["\<\
A handle that has been closed is no longer in the Rust-side registry:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b5dbec9d-00c1-44d2-9335-e454e5ae8855"],

Cell["\<\
With[{c = DuckDBConnect[]},
    DuckDBDisconnect[c];
    DuckDBQuery[c, \"SELECT 1\"]
]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"ddc2c26e-c117-4024-a231-b09d081f7a15"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The raw library", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"6b3f1099-7aac-4078-8a14-27a0171712f9"],

Cell["\<\
The wrappers in Kernel/WolframExample.wl are a thin layer over an association \
of LibraryFunctions, keyed \"namespace::rust_name\". Anything without a \
wrapper is still one lookup away.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"dfdff56a-32a5-4bfe-9e69-88aec0224aa4"],

Cell["Keys[WolframExampleFunctions[]]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e2310de1-9d7f-4595-a569-a829087ffd6c"],

Cell["\<\
inactive_sum builds an expression in Rust and returns it unevaluated:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b584df1b-c006-4c65-b9b0-81a25d2c20c9"],

Cell["WolframExampleFunctions[][\"math::inactive_sum\"][{1, 2, 3}]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"7a9900ce-375e-4751-8944-df690f389d0f"],

Cell["\<\
Activate[WolframExampleFunctions[][\"math::inactive_sum\"][{1, 2, 3}]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"1c4950fd-a388-401a-9cb0-4540fca583f2"],

Cell["\<\
A panic on the Rust side is caught at the boundary and returned as a Failure \
\[LongDash] it does not take the kernel down:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"099d91c7-6dc8-4ff7-a6ce-2c1d2d2efa3d"],

Cell["WolframExampleFunctions[][\"math::panic\"][]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a9bea1b9-c7e3-418f-a38b-9d7c6233e777"],

Cell["\<\
And the signatures of everything that was packaged, straight from the \
generated Artifacts.wl asset:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"79701c63-6f66-40f9-8565-44085483cb65"],

Cell["\<\
Get[PacletObject[\"WolframExampleLib\"][\"AssetLocation\", \"Artifacts\"]] //
    Map[Query[{\"Name\", \"Signatures\"}]] // Dataset\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"34030e6e-056c-4b9a-b950-5c3e0abd7680"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Licensing", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b884d057-8d46-4662-bf47-0ac4e5eef32b"],

Cell["\<\
Everything the library links is compiled into the dylib, so the paclet has to \
carry its own attribution. cargo wl build writes that out as a third asset, \
License.wl: one entry per Rust package in the resolved dependency graph, \
dev-dependencies excluded.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c64cb532-02ab-4469-a61a-ac2c787e7356"],

Cell["\<\
licenses = Get[PacletObject[\"WolframExampleLib\"][\"AssetLocation\", \
\"License\"]];
Length[licenses]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a926ad78-01dd-4a18-880c-41b4c61a57ba"],

Cell["\<\
Each entry carries what the crate's own Cargo.toml declared \[LongDash] \
undeclared fields come back as Missing[\"NotAvailable\"]:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1c4c4da7-bc8c-4053-aec5-390da85fc7b9"],

Cell["Dataset[Take[licenses, 8]]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"abb90620-4b1e-49d2-9e96-bf1044f30c48"],

Cell["\<\
Which licenses the paclet actually ships under, most common first. The values \
are SPDX expressions, so an OR means the crate lets you pick:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b1c4fbea-2a41-4064-a8f0-0adb27ad5f22"],

Cell["ReverseSort[Counts[licenses[[All, \"License\"]]]] // Dataset", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"49a0e103-0181-474a-8443-b191aec9012d"],

Cell["\<\
The entries worth a human's attention \[LongDash] anything that declared no \
license at all. Here that is the two example crates themselves: neither \
Libs/math nor Libs/duckdb sets a license field, which a paclet you actually \
shipped would want to fix:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1efe17e7-9b6a-4412-ade5-54e059b81554"],

Cell["Select[licenses, MissingQ[#[\"License\"]] &] // Dataset", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"af593bc2-fc45-4965-9bc4-2c8188607f70"],

Cell["\<\
And the direct dependencies of the two example crates, as opposed to the \
whole transitive graph:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e869459f-58e1-40df-b77f-92acdc57c748"],

Cell["\<\
Select[licenses,
    MemberQ[{\"duckdb\", \"arrow-ipc\", \"uuid\", \"wolfram-expr\",
             \"wolfram-export\", \"wolfram-serialize\", \
\"wolfram-library-link\"},
        #[\"Name\"]] &] // Dataset\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"5cfe7e46-6bde-444f-b482-5c1796544877"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Rebuilding", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"71e191e0-ea9e-4280-a1bc-aa4b753120e4"],

Cell["\<\
After editing the Rust source, rebuild from the paclet directory. The dylibs \
are content-hashed, so a rebuilt library gets a new file name \[LongDash] \
quit the kernel to pick it up.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ec12798a-b9a7-46c0-a0b5-8dbd606c217f"],

Cell["\<\
ExternalEvaluate[{\"Shell\",
    \"ProcessDirectory\" -> \
FileNameJoin[{ParentDirectory[NotebookDirectory[]], \"WolframExample\"}],
    \"ProcessEnvironment\" -> <|\"PATH\" -> Environment[\"PATH\"]|>},
   \"cargo wl build --release\"]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"027482ab-0684-4175-b4ce-c57532973a95"]
}, Open  ]]
},
WindowTitle->"WolframExample Tour",
FrontEndVersion->"15.0 for Mac OS X ARM (64-bit) (May 6, 2026)",
StyleDefinitions->"Default.nb",
ExpressionUUID->"89f6a343-ad68-4e95-bda4-6bdbc3b45a2a"
]
